tic continuation to the critical strip encodes the poles of $1/\\zeta(s)$. The spectral instabilities (where the state sum diverges) correspond exactly to the non-trivial zeros of the bosonic partition function.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Implications for the Operator Construction\n\nFraming the Riemann zeros as the poles of the fermionic partition function alters the requirements for the Hilbert-Pólya operator:\n\n1.  **From Zeros to Poles:** Instead of constructing a self-adjoint operator whose spectrum directly equals the zeros, the task becomes identifying an operator whose thermodynamic partition function (or Fredholm determinant) has poles at those values.\n2.  **The Role of the GNS Vacuum:** In this swapped representation, the GNS representation of the KMS state acts as a regularizer. The $V_4$ symmetry (the Klein bottle sewing) ensuring that $\\text{Tr}(\\Gamma \\cdot \\rho) \u003d 0$ at the flat boundary corresponds to the requirement that the fermionic vacuum has zero net chiral charge.\n3.  **Self-Adjointness and Reality:** Because the eigenvalues of the modular Hamiltonian on the critical line are related to the real-valued Möbius weights, the self-duality of the natural cone $P^\\natural$ (via the Tomita conjugation $J$) guarantees that the corresponding transfer operator preserves the symmetry across the critical line $\\text{Re}(s) \u003d 1/2$.",
