      "text": "In functional analysis, Fredholm theory establishes a precise, dictionary-like correspondence between the zeros of the Fredholm determinant, the eigenvalues of the operator, and the poles of its resolvent. \n\nFor a trace-class (Schatten class $S_1$) operator $A$ acting on a Hilbert space $\\mathcal{H}$, this relationship is formulated through the following spectral properties:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Fredholm Determinant and Eigenvalues\n\nFor a trace-class operator $A$, the **Fredholm determinant** is defined as an entire function of $z \\in \\mathbb{C}$:\n$$ D(z) \u003d \\det(I - zA) \u003d \\prod_{i\u003d1}^\\infty (1 - z\\lambda_i) $$\nwhere $\\{\\lambda_i\\}$ are the eigenvalues of $A$ counted with their algebraic multiplicities.\n\n*   **The Correspondence:** $z_0 \\in \\mathbb{C}$ is a zero of the entire function $D(z)$ of multiplicity $m$ if and only if $\\lambda_0 \u003d 1/z_0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ of algebraic multiplicity $m$.\n*   **Fredholm Alternative:** The operator $(I - zA)$ is invertible if and only if $\\det(I - zA) \\neq 0$. If $\\det(I - zA) \u003d 0$, then $(I - zA)$ has a non-trivial kernel, meaning $1/z$ is in the point spectrum of $A$.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Resolvent and Fredholm Minors\n\nThe **resolvent** of the operator $A$ parameterized by $z$ is given by:\n$$ R(z; A) \u003d (I - zA)^{-1} $$\nFor a compact operator $A$, $R(z; A)$ is a meromorphic operator-valued function on $\\mathbb{C}$, with poles occurring only at the reciprocals of the eigenvalues of $A$.\n\nFredholm\u0027s fundamental formula expresses this resolvent as the ratio of an entire operator-valued function (the Fredholm minor or numerator) and the scalar Fredholm determinant:\n$$ (I - zA)^{-1} \u003d \\frac{N(z)}{\\det(I - zA)} $$\nwhere $N(z)$ is an entire operator-valued function constructed via the classical Fredholm algebra.\n\n*   **Poles of the Resolvent:** Because the numerator $N(z)$ is entire, the poles of the resolvent $R(z; A)$ are strictly determined by the zeros of the denominator. Specifically, $z_0$ is a pole of the resolvent of order $k$ if and only if $z_0$ is a zero of $\\det(I - zA)$ (and thus $1/z_0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ whose maximal Jordan block has dimension $k$).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Application to the Transfer Operator and Riemann Zeros\n\nWhen this functional analytic framework is mapped to the statistical mechanics of the Primon gas, the operator $A$ is chosen to be the transfer operator (or the scaled modular operator) $e^{-H}$:\n\n1.  **The Fredholm Determinant:**\n    $$ D(s) \u003d \\det(I - e^{-sH}) \u003d \\frac{1}{\\zeta(s)} \\quad (\\text{for } \\text{Re}(s) \u003e 1) $$\n2.  **The Resolvent:** \n    The thermodynamic resolvent is:\n    $$ R(s) \u003d (I - e^{-sH})^{-1} $$\n3.  **The Spectral Correspondence:**\n    *   The **poles of the resolvent** $R(s)$ are the poles of the meromorphic continuation of $(I - e^{-sH})^{-1}$.\n    *   By Fredholm\u0027s formula, these poles are the zeros of the determinant $D(s) \u003d 0$.\n    *   Since $D(s) \u003d 1/\\zeta(s)$, the zeros of the determinant correspond exactly to the singularities of $1/\\zeta(s) \\to \\infty$, which are the **non-trivial zeros of the Riemann Zeta function** $\\zeta(s) \u003d 0$.\n\nUnder this mapping, the imaginary parts of the Riemann zeros are the poles of the resolvent of the modular transfer operator. The Hilbert-Pólya thesis is thus naturally realized not by searching for eigenvalues of a self-adjoint operator on the boundary, but by analyzing the poles of the meromorphic resolvent of the trace-class transfer operator $e^{-H}$ under the Fenchel-dual modular conjugation $J$.",
