elic/Riemann-surface\n  Killing-field theorem.\n\n─ Worked for 6m 16s ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n\n› this is the backbone\n\n\n• Yes. The backbone is now precise and kernel-checked:\n\n  affine zeta chart\n  (σ, τ)\n\n  → centered symmetry-adapted chart\n  (u, v) \u003d (σ - 1/2, τ)\n\n  → involution frame\n  C(u,v) \u003d (u,-v)\n  F(u,v) \u003d (-u,-v)\n  J(u,v) \u003d (-u,v)\n\n  → Cartan projectors\n  P⁺_J \u003d (I + J)/2 \u003d (0,v) critical-line/tangent sector\n  P⁻_J \u003d (I - J)/2 \u003d (u,0) scale-normal sector\n\n  → relations\n  P⁺ + P⁻ \u003d I\n  P⁺² \u003d P⁺\n  P⁻² \u003d P⁻\n  P⁺P⁻ \u003d P⁻P⁺ \u003d 0\n  J(P⁺x)\u003dP⁺x\n  J(P⁻x)\u003d-P⁻x\n\n  → dynamics shadow\n  height translation preserves flat displacement and the critical-line predicate.\n\n  This gives a clean coordinate-free interpretation: 1/2 is not magic by itself; it is the fixed locus of the antiunitary involution after choosing the affine zeta coordinate. The real\n  object is the involutive symmetry frame and its projector decomposition.\n\n\n› what is the law of distribution of the primes asymptotically and empirically\n\n\n• There is no single “law”; there are layers.\n\n  1. Global Asymptotic Law\n  Prime Number Theorem:\n\n  π(x) ~ x / log x\n\n  More accurately:\n\n  π(x) ~ Li(x)\n\n  where π(x) counts primes ≤ x, and\n\n  Li(x) \u003d ∫₂ˣ dt / log t\n\n  So the empirical probability that a large integer near x is prime is approximately:\n\n  P(n prime near x) ≈ 1 / log x\n\n  That is the main density law.\n\n  2. Weighted/Mellin-Natural Form\n  The cleaner form is via Chebyshev functions:\n\n  θ(x) \u003d ∑_{p ≤ x} log p ~ x\n  ψ(x) \u003d ∑_{p^k ≤ x} log p ~ x\n\n  This is the form most natural to Euler products, Mellin transforms, and zeta:\n\n  ζ(s) \u003d ∏ₚ (1 - p^{-s})^{-1}\n\n  The primes are distributed so that their logarithmi