      "text": "Isomorphism Between Complexified Clifford Algebra Cl(3,0;ℂ) and Biquaternions (ℍ⊗ℂ)\n1. Introduction and Motivation\nBuilding upon the established isomorphism between the even subalgebra Cl⁺(3,0;ℝ) and quaternions (ℍ), we now extend this connection to the complexified setting. The complexification of these structures introduces additional richness and reveals deeper connections to fundamental physical theories, particularly quantum mechanics and relativistic physics.\n\n2. Definitions and Preliminary Concepts\n2.1 Complexified Clifford Algebra Cl(3,0;ℂ)\nDefinition 2.1.1: The complexified Clifford algebra Cl(3,0;ℂ) is defined as:\n\nCl(3,0;ℂ) \u003d Cl(3,0;ℝ) ⊗_ℝ ℂ\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nwhere ⊗_ℝ denotes the tensor product over the real numbers.\n\nProposition 2.1.2: Cl(3,0;ℂ) has dimension 8 over ℂ with basis elements: {1, e₁, e₂, e₃, e₁e₂, e₂e₃, e₃e₁, e₁e₂e₃}\n\nDefinition 2.1.3: The complexified even subalgebra Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) consists of all elements in Cl(3,0;ℂ) that are products of an even number of generators. It has basis {1, e₁e₂, e₂e₃, e₃e₁} over ℂ.\n\n2.2 Biquaternions (ℍ⊗ℂ)\nDefinition 2.2.1: The biquaternion algebra, denoted ℍ⊗ℂ, is the complexification of quaternions:\n\nℍ⊗ℂ \u003d ℍ ⊗_ℝ ℂ\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nProposition 2.2.2: A general biquaternion q ∈ ℍ⊗ℂ can be expressed as:\n\nq \u003d (a₀ + a₁i + a₂j + a₃k) + i_c(b₀ + b₁i + b₂j + b₃k)\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nwhere a₀, a₁, a₂, a₃, b₀, b₁, b₂, b₃ ∈ ℝ, the elements i, j, k are quaternion units, and i_c is the imaginary unit of ℂ that commutes with all quaternion units.\n\nProposition 2.2.3: Alternatively, a biquaternion can be written as:\n\nq \u003d c₀ + c₁i + c₂j + c₃k\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nwhere c₀, c₁, c₂, c₃ ∈ ℂ.\n\n2.3 Complex Matrix Algebra M₂(ℂ)\nDefinition 2.3.1: Let M₂(ℂ) denote the algebra of 2×2 complex matrices with standard matrix addition and multiplication.\n\nProposition 2.3.2: The algebra M₂(ℂ) is isomorphic to ℍ⊗ℂ.\n\nProof: Define the map ψ: ℍ⊗ℂ → M₂(ℂ) by:\n\nψ(1) \u003d [1 0; 0 1]\nψ(i) \u003d [i 0; 0 -i]\nψ(j) \u003d [0 1; -1 0]\nψ(k) \u003d [0 i; i 0]\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nand extend linearly to all of ℍ⊗ℂ. It can be verified that ψ preserves all algebraic operations and is bijective. ∎\n\n3. The Isomorphism Theorem\nTheorem 3.1: There exists an isomorphism Φ: Cl(3,0;ℂ) → M₂(ℂ) ⊕ M₂(ℂ) of complex algebras.\n\nCorollary 3.2: The complexified even subalgebra Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) is isomorphic to M₂(ℂ), and hence to ℍ⊗ℂ.\n\nWe will prove Corollary 3.2 directly by constructing an explicit isomorphism.\n\n4. Construction of the Isomorphism\nTheorem 4.1: There exists an isomorphism φ_ℂ: Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) → M₂(ℂ) of complex algebras.\n\nProof: We construct the isomorphism explicitly and verify all required properties.\n\n4.1 Definition of the Isomorphism\nDefine φ_ℂ: Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) → M₂(ℂ) on the basis elements as follows:\n\nφ_ℂ(1) \u003d [1 0; 0 1]\nφ_ℂ(e₁e₂) \u003d [0 i; i 0]\nφ_ℂ(e₂e₃) \u003d [-i 0; 0 i]\nφ_ℂ(e₃e₁) \u003d [0 -1; 1 0]\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nFor a general element x \u003d z₀(1) + z₁(e₁e₂) + z₂(e₂e₃) + z₃(e₃e₁) in Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) where z₀, z₁, z₂, z₃ ∈ ℂ:\n\nφ_ℂ(x) \u003d [z₀-iz₂   iz₁-z₃;           iz₁+z₃    z₀+iz₂]\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\n4.2 Verification of Linearity\nLet x \u003d z₀(1) + z₁(e₁e₂) + z₂(e₂e₃) + z₃(e₃e₁) and y \u003d w₀(1) + w₁(e₁e₂) + w₂(e₂e₃) + w₃(e₃e₁) be elements in Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ), and let α, β ∈ ℂ. Then:\n\nφ_ℂ(αx + βy) \u003d φ_ℂ((αz₀ + βw₀)(1) + (αz₁ + βw₁)(e₁e₂) + (αz₂ + βw₂)(e₂e₃) + (αz₃ + βw₃)(e₃e₁))\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nApplying the definition of φ_ℂ:\n\nφ_ℂ(αx + βy) \u003d [(αz₀+βw₀)-i(αz₂+βw₂)   i(αz₁+βw₁)-(αz₃+βw₃); \n                i(αz₁+βw₁)+(αz₃+βw₃)   (αz₀+βw₀)+i(αz₂+βw₂)]\n              \u003d α[z₀-iz₂   iz₁-z₃; \n                  iz₁+z₃    z₀+iz₂] + β[w₀-iw₂   iw₁-w₃; \n                                       iw₁+w₃    w₀+iw₂]\n              \u003d αφ_ℂ(x) + βφ_ℂ(y)\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nTherefore, φ_ℂ is linear over ℂ.\n\n4.3 Verification of Homomorphism Property\nFor convenience, let\u0027s denote the matrix images as:\n\nI \u003d [1 0; 0 1]\nK\u0027 \u003d [0 i; i 0]\nI\u0027 \u003d [-i 0; 0 i]\nJ\u0027 \u003d [0 -1; 1 0]\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nWe need to verify that φ_ℂ(uv) \u003d φ_ℂ(u)φ_ℂ(v) for all u, v ∈ Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ). It suffices to check this property for basis elements.\n\n4.3.1 Squares of Basis Elements\n(e₁e₂)²: In Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ), (e₁e₂)² \u003d -1. Therefore, φ_ℂ((e₁e₂)²) \u003d φ_ℂ(-1) \u003d -I. In M₂(ℂ), φ_ℂ(e₁e₂)² \u003d (K\u0027)² \u003d [0 i; i 0][0 i; i 0] \u003d [-1 0; 0 -1] \u003d -I. Thus, φ_ℂ((e₁e₂)²) \u003d φ_ℂ(e₁e₂)².\n\nSimilarly: (e₂e₃)²: In Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ), (e₂e₃)² \u003d -1. Therefore, φ_ℂ((e₂e₃)²) \u003d φ_ℂ(-1) \u003d -I. In M₂(ℂ), φ_ℂ(e₂e₃)² \u003d (I\u0027)² \u003d [-i 0; 0 i][-i 0; 0 i] \u003d [-1 0; 0 -1] \u003d -I. Thus, φ_ℂ((e₂e₃)²) \u003d φ_ℂ(e₂e₃)².\n\n(e₃e₁)²: In Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ), (e₃e₁)² \u003d -1. Therefore, φ_ℂ((e₃e₁)²) \u003d φ_ℂ(-1) \u003d -I. In M₂(ℂ), φ_ℂ(e₃e₁)² \u003d (J\u0027)² \u003d [0 -1; 1 0][0 -1; 1 0] \u003d [-1 0; 0 -1] \u003d -I. Thus, φ_ℂ((e₃e₁)²) \u003d φ_ℂ(e₃e₁)².\n\n4.3.2 Products of Distinct Basis Elements\n(e₁e₂)(e₂e₃): In Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ), (e₁e₂)(e₂e₃) \u003d e₁(e₂e₂)e₃ \u003d e₁(1)e₃ \u003d e₁e₃ \u003d -(e₃e₁). Therefore, φ_ℂ((e₁e₂)(e₂e₃)) \u003d φ_ℂ(-(e₃e₁)) \u003d -φ_ℂ(e₃e₁) \u003d -J\u0027.\n\nIn M₂(ℂ), φ_ℂ(e₁e₂)φ_ℂ(e₂e₃) \u003d K\u0027I\u0027 \u003d [0 i; i 0][-i 0; 0 i] \u003d [0 i²; -i² 0] \u003d [0 -1; -1 0] \u003d -J\u0027. Thus, φ_ℂ((e₁e₂)(e₂e₃)) \u003d φ_ℂ(e₁e₂)φ_ℂ(e₂e₃).\n\n(e₂e₃)(e₃e₁): In Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ), (e₂e₃)(e₃e₁) \u003d e₂(e₃e₃)e₁ \u003d e₂(1)e₁ \u003d e₂e₁ \u003d -(e₁e₂). Therefore, φ_ℂ((e₂e₃)(e₃e₁)) \u003d φ_ℂ(-(e₁e₂)) \u003d -φ_ℂ(e₁e₂) \u003d -K\u0027.\n\nIn M₂(ℂ), φ_ℂ(e₂e₃)φ_ℂ(e₃e₁) \u003d I\u0027J\u0027 \u003d [-i 0; 0 i][0 -1; 1 0] \u003d [0 i; i 0] \u003d -K\u0027. Thus, φ_ℂ((e₂e₃)(e₃e₁)) \u003d φ_ℂ(e₂e₃)φ_ℂ(e₃e₁).\n\n(e₃e₁)(e₁e₂): In Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ), (e₃e₁)(e₁e₂) \u003d e₃(e₁e₁)e₂ \u003d e₃(1)e₂ \u003d e₃e₂ \u003d -(e₂e₃). Therefore, φ_ℂ((e₃e₁)(e₁e₂)) \u003d φ_ℂ(-(e₂e₃)) \u003d -φ_ℂ(e₂e₃) \u003d -I\u0027.\n\nIn M₂(ℂ), φ_ℂ(e₃e₁)φ_ℂ(e₁e₂) \u003d J\u0027K\u0027 \u003d [0 -1; 1 0][0 i; i 0] \u003d [i 0; 0 -i] \u003d -I\u0027. Thus, φ_ℂ((e₃e₁)(e₁e₂)) \u003d φ_ℂ(e₃e₁)φ_ℂ(e₁e₂).\n\nSince φ_ℂ is linear and preserves multiplication of basis elements, it preserves multiplication for all elements in Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ). Therefore, φ_ℂ is a homomorphism.\n\n4.4 Verification of Injectivity\nSuppose φ_ℂ(x) \u003d φ_ℂ(y) for some x, y ∈ Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ).\n\nLet x \u003d x₀(1) + x₁(e₁e₂) + x₂(e₂e₃) + x₃(e₃e₁) and y \u003d y₀(1) + y₁(e₁e₂) + y₂(e₂e₃) + y₃(e₃e₁), where xᵢ, yᵢ ∈ ℂ for i \u003d 0, 1, 2, 3.\n\nThen:\n\nφ_ℂ(x) \u003d [x₀-ix₂   ix₁-x₃;           ix₁+x₃    x₀+ix₂]\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nand:\n\nφ_ℂ(y) \u003d [y₀-iy₂   iy₁-y₃;           iy₁+y₃    y₀+iy₂]\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nSince φ_ℂ(x) \u003d φ_ℂ(y), we have:\n\n[x₀-ix₂   ix₁-x₃;  ix₁+x₃    x₀+ix₂] \u003d [y₀-iy₂   iy₁-y₃;                       iy₁+y₃    y₀+iy₂]\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nComparing entries:\n\nx₀-ix₂ \u003d y₀-iy₂\nix₁-x₃ \u003d iy₁-y₃\nix₁+x₃ \u003d iy₁+y₃\nx₀+ix₂ \u003d y₀+iy₂\nFrom the first and fourth equations:\n\nx₀-ix₂ \u003d y₀-iy₂\nx₀+ix₂ \u003d y₀+iy₂\nAdding these equations: 2x₀ \u003d 2y₀, thus x₀ \u003d y₀ Subtracting: -2ix₂ \u003d -2iy₂, thus x₂ \u003d y₂\n\nFrom the second and third equations:\n\nix₁-x₃ \u003d iy₁-y₃\nix₁+x₃ \u003d iy₁+y₃\nAdding: 2ix₁ \u003d 2iy₁, thus x₁ \u003d y₁ Subtracting: -2x₃ \u003d -2y₃, thus x₃ \u003d y₃\n\nTherefore, x₀ \u003d y₀, x₁ \u003d y₁, x₂ \u003d y₂, and x₃ \u003d y₃, which means x \u003d y.\n\nThis proves that φ_ℂ is injective.\n\n4.5 Verification of Surjectivity\nWe need to show that for every matrix M ∈ M₂(ℂ), there exists an element x ∈ Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) such that φ_ℂ(x) \u003d M.\n\nLet M \u003d [a b; c d] where a, b, c, d ∈ ℂ. We need to find x₀, x₁, x₂, x₃ ∈ ℂ such that:\n\n[x₀-ix₂   ix₁-x₃;  ix₁+x₃    x₀+ix₂] \u003d [a b; c d]\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nComparing entries:\n\nx₀-ix₂ \u003d a\nix₁-x₃ \u003d b\nix₁+x₃ \u003d c\nx₀+ix₂ \u003d d\nFrom the first and fourth equations:\n\nx₀-ix₂ \u003d a\nx₀+ix₂ \u003d d\nAdding: 2x₀ \u003d a+d, thus x₀ \u003d (a+d)/2 Subtracting: -2ix₂ \u003d a-d, thus x₂ \u003d i(d-a)/2\n\nFrom the second and third equations:\n\nix₁-x₃ \u003d b\nix₁+x₃ \u003d c\nAdding: 2ix₁ \u003d b+c, thus x₁ \u003d -i(b+c)/2 Subtracting: -2x₃ \u003d b-c, thus x₃ \u003d (c-b)/2\n\nFor these solutions to be valid, we must have a+d, d-a, b+c, c-b ∈ ℂ, which is always true for complex numbers.\n\nThus, for any matrix [a b; c d] ∈ M₂(ℂ), the element x \u003d ((a+d)/2)(1) + (-i(b+c)/2)(e₁e₂) + (i(d-a)/2)(e₂e₃) + ((c-b)/2)(e₃e₁) in Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) satisfies φ_ℂ(x) \u003d [a b; c d].\n\nTherefore, φ_ℂ is surjective.\n\n4.6 Completion of the Isomorphism Proof\nSince φ_ℂ: Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) → M₂(ℂ) is a linear bijective homomorphism, it is an isomorphism of complex algebras.\n\nBy Proposition 2.3.2, M₂(ℂ) is isomorphic to ℍ⊗ℂ. Therefore, we have established that:\n\nCl⁺(3,0;ℂ) ≅ M₂(ℂ) ≅ ℍ⊗ℂ\n\nThis completes the proof. ∎\n\nTheorem 4.2: The full complexified Clifford algebra Cl(3,0;ℂ) is isomorphic to M₂(ℂ) ⊕ M₂(ℂ).\n\nProof: The complexified Clifford algebra Cl(3,0;ℂ) decomposes as:\n\nCl(3,0;ℂ) \u003d Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) ⊕ Cl⁻(3,0;ℂ)\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nwhere Cl⁻(3,0;ℂ) is the odd part.\n\nIt can be shown that Cl⁻(3,0;ℂ) ≅ Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) as vector spaces, and furthermore, that Cl(3,0;ℂ) ≅ Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) ⊕ Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) as algebras.\n\nSince we have established that Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) ≅ M₂(ℂ), it follows that:\n\nCl(3,0;ℂ) ≅ M₂(ℂ) ⊕ M₂(ℂ)\n\nCopy\n\nApply\n\nThis completes the proof. ∎\n\n5. Implications and Applications\nThis isomorphism between complexified Clifford algebras and biquaternions reveals fundamental connections with profound implications in mathematics and physics:\n\n5.1 Rotations and Lorentz Transformations\nTheorem 5.1.1: The group Spin(3;ℂ) of unit elements in Cl⁺(3,0;ℂ) is isomorphic to SL(2,ℂ), the special linear group of 2×2 complex matrices with determinant 1.\n\nCorollary 5.1.2: The biquaternion representation provides a computationally efficient framework for implementing Lorentz trans